Grade 1 acromioclavicular joint sprain from fall onto shoulder to shoulder
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Focal AC joint tenderness, Pain on cross-body adduction, Normal contour without step-off deformity

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-002
Reason for Visit: Acute severe shoulder pain following a mechanical fall, requiring assessment for fracture or dislocation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified severe acute shoulder pain post-trauma limiting upper limb function. Urgent clinical assessment was necessary to rule out glenohumeral dislocation or clavicular fracture and prevent joint compromise prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I fell directly on my shoulder and now I cannot lift my arm across my chest without sharp pain.
History of Present Illness: Injury occurred following a direct fall onto the lateral aspect of the adducted shoulder. Patient experiences focal point tenderness directly over the acromioclavicular joint space. Pain is reproduced during active and passive cross-body adduction, and exacerbates upon active shoulder elevation above 90 degrees. No severe ecchymosis is present in the acute phase.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently unable to use the affected upper limb for overhead tasks.
Social/Safety Concerns: Impaired dominant hand function limits activities of daily living and self-care.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Alert, holding the affected arm adducted and internally rotated against the abdomen for comfort.
Physical Exam: Shoulder examination reveals focal AC joint tenderness and mild localized swelling. Structural inspection shows a normal contour without step-off deformity. Pain is elicited on cross-body adduction. Intact glenohumeral range of motion with only terminal restriction due to pain. Negative O'Brien's active compression test. Distal clavicle remains stable upon anterior-posterior manual translation.
Environmental Observations: Slippery rug identified at the site of the fall; hazard removed.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications. Paracetamol taken 2 hours ago with minimal effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Grade 1 acromioclavicular joint sprain - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Clavicular fracture - Ruled out due to: Absence of mid-shaft bony crepitus, severe deformity, or extreme focal shaft tenderness.

Glenohumeral dislocation - Ruled out due to: Intact shoulder contour, negative apprehension tests, and preserved passive range of motion.

Rotator cuff tear - Ruled out due to: Preserved strength against resistance below 90 degrees and negative drop arm test.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The mechanism of injury and acute functional deficit prompted urgent concern for a fracture or dislocation. On-site assessment confidently isolated the pathology to a Grade 1 acromioclavicular joint sprain, devoid of gross instability. This urgent after-hours evaluation was essential to safely clear major structural trauma, initiate appropriate sling immobilization, and provide definitive analgesia, thereby preventing unnecessary emergency department transfer and safely managing the patient until daytime radiography and GP follow-up.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Broad arm sling provided for comfort. Prescribed short course of NSAIDs for local inflammation. Advised standard AP and Zanca view shoulder radiographs if symptoms fail to improve.
Positive Behavioral Change: Utilize the sling for 48 hours to offload the AC joint, which will rapidly decrease acute pain. Gradually wean from the sling as comfort allows to prevent secondary adhesive capsulitis.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Broad arm sling.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 to 5 days for clinical reassessment and potential physiotherapy referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Attend the Emergency Department if you develop progressive swelling, noticeable visual deformity of the collarbone, numbness, or color changes in the hand.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending potential radiological investigations; conditions may evolve.
Clinical Course: Expect acute pain for 3 to 5 days, followed by gradual improvement over 2 to 4 weeks. Unrestricted overhead activity may take 6 weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate care if your hand becomes cold or numb, or if a physical bump suddenly appears over your collarbone.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The sling removes the weight of the arm from the injured joint ligaments, allowing the initial inflammatory phase to settle.
Actionable Steps: Rest the arm. Use ice packs for 15 minutes every 2 hours. Perform regular finger and wrist movements to maintain circulation.

